$DIR/main.p:7:7: a function literal that captures variables cannot be started with a task statement: a lives in the starting frame, which may be gone before the task runs (spec section 12.12); a has type [2]atomic.Int32, which holds a value of type atomic.Int32 and must not be copied, so start the literal with a sync.Scope, which waits for it and lets it use a: var s sync.Scope; s.Task(func() {...})
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it reaches
    them in that function's stack frame. A task statement may start it after
    that function has returned, when the frame is gone.
    Start the literal with a sync.Scope instead: s.Task(func() {...}) waits for
    it before s's block ends, so it may use the function's variables, a Mutex or
    a WaitGroup among them. Or pass the values the literal needs as arguments:
    they are copied when the task statement runs. Do not pass a Mutex, a
    WaitGroup or another sync type this way, since the copy is a different one.
    The compiler reports this where it sees the literal itself: written in the
    task statement, or held by a local variable whose only assignment is its
    declaration. Library functions that start a task with a func argument, such
    as sync.WaitGroup.Task and time.AfterFunc, follow the same rule, as does a
    function of the program that passes a func parameter on to a task. A method
    value is E2594.
    a holds a value of type atomic.Int32. Passing it as an argument would copy
    it, and a copy is a separate value: calling Done or Unlock on the copy never
    reaches the original, so a Wait or Lock on the original may never return. A
    sync.Scope declared in this function waits for the tasks it starts before
    its block ends, so they may use a where it is.
    Go moves captured variables to the heap. Plutonium never allocates behind
    the program's back, so captured variables stay in their frame (spec section
    11.5).
    Start the literal with a sync.Scope:
        var s sync.Scope
        s.Task(func() {
            ...
        })
    Or declare it at package level and pass a pointer:
        var a [2]atomic.Int32 // at package level
        task func(a *[2]atomic.Int32) {
            ...
        }(&a)
    (see "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12)
    (run "pluto explain E2590" for a longer explanation)
